The sample proportion is  

\[
\hat p=\frac{x}{n}= \frac{28}{400}=0.07 .
\]

Under the null hypothesis \(p_0 = 0.05\), the standard error is  

\[
SE = \sqrt{\frac{p_0(1-p_0)}{n}}
     = \sqrt{\frac{0.05\cdot0.95}{400}}
     = \sqrt{\frac{0.0475}{400}}
     = \sqrt{0.00011875}
     \approx 0.010902 .
\]

The z‑statistic (using the normal approximation without continuity correction) is  

\[
z = \frac{\hat p - p_0}{SE}
    = \frac{0.07 - 0.05}{0.010902}
    \approx \frac{0.02}{0.010902}
    \approx 1.834 .
\]

**FINAL ANSWER:** 1.834 (rounded to three decimal places)

The Welch two‑sample t statistic is  

\[
t=\frac{\bar x_1-\bar x_2}{\sqrt{\dfrac{s_1^{2}}{n_1}+\dfrac{s_2^{2}}{n_2}}}
   =\frac{24.5-21.0}{\sqrt{\dfrac{1.8^{2}}{12}+\dfrac{4.2^{2}}{25}}}
